R438	Addressee Arg1:T307 Arg2:T313	
R439	Speaker Arg1:T302 Arg2:T313	
T314	Quotation 28356 28388	"Why did you beat the pavement?"
R440	Speaker Arg1:T307 Arg2:T314	
R441	Addressee Arg1:T302 Arg2:T314	
T315	Quotation 28389 28579	"My dear doctor, this is a time for observation, not for talk. We are spies in an enemy's country. We know something of Saxe-Coburg Square. Let us now explore the parts which lie behind it."
R442	Addressee Arg1:T307 Arg2:T315	
R443	Speaker Arg1:T302 Arg2:T315	
T316	Quotation 29235 29248	"Let me see,"
T317	Cue 29249 29253	said
T318	Entity 29254 29260	Holmes
R444	Cueing Arg1:T317 Arg2:T316	
R445	Speaker Arg1:T318 Arg2:T316	
R446	Addressee Arg1:T307 Arg2:T316	
T319	Quotation 29314 29911	"I should like just to remember the order of the houses here. It is a hobby of mine to have an exact knowledge of London. There is Mortimer's, the tobacconist, the little newspaper shop, the Coburg branch of the City and Suburban Bank, the Vegetarian Restaurant, and McFarlane's carriage-building depot. That carries us right on to the other block. And now, Doctor, we've done our work, so it's time we had some play. A sandwich and a cup of coffee, and then off to violin-land, where all is sweetness and delicacy and harmony, and there are no red-headed clients to vex us with their conundrums."
R447	Cueing Arg1:T317 Arg2:T319	
R448	Speaker Arg1:T318 Arg2:T319	
R449	Addressee Arg1:T307 Arg2:T319	
T320	Quotation 31319 31359	"You want to go home, no doubt, Doctor,"
T321	Entity 31360 31362	he
T322	Cue 31363 31371	remarked
R450	Speaker Arg1:T321 Arg2:T320	
R451	Cueing Arg1:T322 Arg2:T320	
T324	Quotation 31387 31414	"Yes, it would be as well."
R453	Addressee Arg1:T321 Arg2:T324	
T323	Entity 31229 31230	I
R452	Addressee Arg1:T323 Arg2:T320	
R454	Speaker Arg1:T323 Arg2:T324	
T325	Quotation 31415 31518	"And I have some business to do which will take some hours. This business at Coburg Square is serious."
R455	Speaker Arg1:T321 Arg2:T325	
R456	Addressee Arg1:T323 Arg2:T325	
